{"from": "site:bethany-beyond-jordan", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-01-28-baptizing-bethany-beyond-jordan", "grade": "B", "note": "Al-Maghtas, on the east bank of the Jordan, contains buildings 'with both aspects of a Jewish mikveh (ritual bath) resembling Second Temple period pools from Qumran' - an excavated east-bank baptism landscape of the period (reused site and sources from Matthew 3)."}
{"from": "site:bethany-beyond-jordan", "type": "complicates", "to": "claim:jn-01-28-baptizing-bethany-beyond-jordan", "note": "Origen, in the 3rd century, noticed 'there was no place called Bethany east of the Jordan' and suggested amending the name to Bethabara - the gospel place-name was already unstable (reused from Matthew 3)."}
{"from": "text:origen-commentary-john-6", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-01-28-baptizing-bethany-beyond-jordan", "grade": "D", "note": "3rd-century reception: Origen preferred 'Bethabara' over 'Bethany' as the location where John was baptizing - a reception text reporting the textual and place tradition, graded D by rule."}
{"from": "artifact:madaba-map", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-01-28-baptizing-bethany-beyond-jordan", "grade": "D", "note": "The 6th-century Madaba Map labels 'Bethabara, the place of St. John's baptising' by the Jordan - Byzantine reception of the baptism-site tradition, not independent 1st-century evidence."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-tamid-7-3", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-01-19-priests-levites-sent", "grade": "C", "note": "The daily Temple service is carried out by priests with 'the Levites recited the psalm of that day' - the same two orders the interrogation presupposes as Temple personnel. Redacted c. 200 CE; it may not describe the practice of the 30s CE exactly."}
{"from": "site:second-temple", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-01-19-priests-levites-sent", "grade": "C", "note": "The Second Temple in Jerusalem was the chief place of worship and communal gathering - the institution whose serving orders (priests and Levites) the interrogation presupposes (reused site and sources from Matthew 5)."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-eduyot-8-7", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-01-20-john-denied-titles", "grade": "C", "note": "Rabbinic tradition expects Elijah to come before the day of the Lord ('Behold I send to you Elijah the prophet...') - the living expectation behind the interrogators' question 'Are you Elijah?' (reused text from Mark 8/Matthew 11). Redacted c. 200 CE and may not describe the practice of the 30s CE exactly."}
{"from": "artifact:great-isaiah-scroll", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-01-23-john-applied-isaiah-40-3", "grade": "B", "note": "The Great Isaiah Scroll (ca. 125 BCE) carries Isaiah 40:3, the verse John applies to himself - the quoted wording existed in Hebrew long before (reused node and sources from Matthew 3/Luke 3)."}
{"from": "site:et-tell", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-01-44-philip-from-bethsaida", "grade": "D", "note": "et-Tell is the long-standing candidate for Bethsaida, 'inhabited on a lesser scale during the first centuries BCE and CE'; the identification is now disputed by el-Araj (reused site and sources from Matthew 11/Luke 9)."}
{"from": "site:el-araj", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-01-44-philip-from-bethsaida", "grade": "D", "note": "el-Araj's excavators report Roman-layer pottery, coins and stone vessels 'typical of first-century Jewish life' on the north shore, arguing it 'a much better candidate for Bethsaida' (reused site and sources)."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-bethsaida", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-01-44-philip-from-bethsaida", "grade": "B", "note": "Josephus names 'the village Bethsaids, situate at the lake of Gennesareth', advanced by Philip the Tetrarch to the dignity of a city and renamed Julias - the named town John 1:44 calls the city of Andrew and Peter (reused from Matthew 11). Regraded A to B by architect review, consistent with Luke 9:10: Josephus names the town (A for the place), not that Philip, Andrew and Peter came from it."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-116-119", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-01-06-john-witness-sent", "grade": "B", "note": "Josephus confirms John was a preacher who drew crowds, taught righteousness and baptized - the historical core of 'a man named John, who came as a witness' (reused text and sources from Matthew 3)."}
{"from": "claim:jn-01-23-john-applied-isaiah-40-3", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-03-03-isaiah-40-3-applied", "note": "Difference: in John the Baptist applies Isaiah 40:3 to himself in answer to interrogators; Matthew 3:3 applies the verse about John, and Mark 1:2-3 pairs it with Malachi 3:1 under Isaiah's name."}
{"from": "claim:jn-01-29-lamb-of-god-acclamation", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-03-13-jesus-baptized", "note": "Difference: at the same Jordan meeting John's Gospel reports the Baptist's acclamation ('Lamb of God') and his testimony that he saw the Spirit descend, instead of Matthew's voice from heaven ('This is my beloved Son') and John's protest 'I need to be baptized by you'."}
{"from": "claim:jn-01-41-42-cephas-naming", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-04-18-fishermen-called", "note": "Difference: John sets the first meeting and the Cephas naming in Judea (Bethany beyond the Jordan), the day after the baptism, after John the Baptist's testimony; Matthew 4:18-20 and Mark 1:16-20 have Jesus call Simon and Andrew while casting nets by the Sea of Galilee. The graph does not identify the two scenes."}
{"from": "claim:jn-01-38-42-aramaic-glosses", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-23-07-title-rabbi", "note": "The same title 'Rabbi' in use; John 1:38 glosses it ('which is to say, Teacher') for Greek readers, Matthew 23 records it as an honorific the scribes and Pharisees love."}
{"from": "claim:jn-01-44-philip-from-bethsaida", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:lk-09-10-city-called-bethsaida", "note": "John 1:44 names Bethsaida as the home city of Andrew, Peter and Philip; Luke 9:10 has Jesus withdraw to a city called Bethsaida - the same named town, complementary uses."}
{"from": "claim:jn-01-44-philip-from-bethsaida", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-11-21-bethsaida", "note": "John 1:44 names Bethsaida as the home city of Andrew, Peter and Philip; Matthew 11:21 has Jesus denounce Bethsaida - the same named town, complementary uses."}
